type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Daniel
    family-names: Martín-Cudero
    affiliation: >-
      Department of Financial Economics and Accounting, Area
      of Mathematics Education, Rey Juan Carlos University,
      Madrid, Spain. 
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0009-0007-5946-6744'
abstract: >-
  Interactive educational resource aimed at Primary and
  Secondary Education students to support the learning of
  divisibility and modular arithmetic through a guided
  learning activity and two complementary interactive
  simulators. The guided activity provides structured tasks
  that encourage students to observe, explore, formulate
  hypotheses, construct, verify graph transitions, and check
  divisibility. The simulators complement this approach by
  allowing students to explore and visualize divisibility
  graphs in different ways. They support both the automatic
  visualization of how a given number is processed digit by
  digit through the graph and the reverse reconstruction of
  numbers from a specified sequence of states, showing the
  digits compatible with each transition and the different
  possible combinations. Together, the three resources
  promote active and experiential learning by making
  mathematical processes that are usually abstract visually
  and dynamically accessible, while strengthening logical
  reasoning, mathematical understanding, conjecture
  formulation, systematic exploration, and independent
  learning.
keywords:
  - divisibility
  - directed graphs
  - modular arithmetic
  - mathematics education
  - educational software
  - interactive mathematics
  - guided learning
